\title{Error-Correcting Neural Codes and the Sphere-Packing Bound\\
for Binary Neural Populations}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a self-contained theory of information capacity for populations of
binary (firing/silent) neurons under the demand of noise tolerance. Modeling a
\emph{neural code} on $N$ neurons as a binary activity pattern in $\{0,1\}^N$, we
first establish the raw representational capacity: exactly $2^N$ distinct
patterns are available. We then ask how much of this capacity survives when the
population must decode reliably in the presence of misfiring neurons. Introducing
the Hamming distance as the natural measure of confusability, we define a
$t$-error-correcting codebook as a finite set of patterns any two of which are at
distance at least $2t+1$. Our central result is the \textbf{sphere-packing
(Hamming) bound}: for any such codebook $C$ on $N$ neurons,
\[
  |C|\cdot\sum_{k=0}^{t}\binom{N}{k}\;\le\;2^N.
\]
The proof rests on three elementary but structurally important facts: (i) the
number of patterns of a given weight is a binomial coefficient, and these
partition the $2^N$ patterns; (ii) Hamming balls are translation invariant under
neuron-wise exclusive-or, so a ball of radius $r$ has volume
$\sum_{k=0}^{r}\binom{N}{k}$ independent of its center; and (iii) the radius-$t$
balls around the codewords of a $t$-error-correcting code are pairwise disjoint,
by the triangle inequality. The bound subsumes the raw capacity result as its
$t=0$ special case and, for single-error correction ($t=1$), yields the concrete
capacity ceiling $|C|\cdot(N+1)\le 2^N$. We discuss the interpretation of these
results as quantitative constraints on distributed population coding in the
brain, present numerical illustrations, and outline extensions that bracket and
refine the optimal code size.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A recurring question at the interface of neuroscience and information theory is
how much information a population of neurons can represent. In the coarsest
useful abstraction, each neuron is a binary unit --- firing ($1$) or silent
($0$) --- and the instantaneous state of $N$ neurons is a pattern in the Boolean
cube $\{0,1\}^N$. The number of such patterns is $2^N$, the \emph{raw capacity}:
an idealized upper limit on the number of mutually distinguishable states a
binary population can occupy.

Raw capacity presumes noiseless readout. Biological neurons are unreliable: they
drop spikes, fire spuriously, and are subject to metabolic and synaptic
fluctuation. If a code assigns meaning to two patterns that differ in a single
neuron, then a single misfire converts one meaning into another with no
possibility of recovery. A code robust to up to $t$ simultaneous neuron faults
must therefore keep its meaningful patterns well separated. Making this precise
leads directly into the classical theory of error-correcting codes, and in
particular to the sphere-packing (Hamming) bound.

The purpose of this paper is a clean, fully explicit development of this bound in
the language of neural populations, with its two most important corollaries: the
recovery of the raw capacity $2^N$ in the noiseless limit, and the exact price
$|C|\le 2^N/(N+1)$ of single-error correction. Every definition, lemma, and
theorem is stated inline in elementary terms; the arguments require nothing
beyond finite counting, a symmetry of the Boolean cube, and the triangle
inequality for Hamming distance.

\section{Preliminaries and notation}

Throughout, $N$ denotes the number of neurons and $t$ a nonnegative integer
error-tolerance parameter. We write $\binom{N}{k}$ for the binomial coefficient,
with $\binom{N}{k}=0$ for $k>N$; $|S|$ for the cardinality of a finite set $S$;
and $[N]=\{0,1,\dots,N-1\}$ for the index set of neurons.

\begin{definition}[Neural code]
A \emph{neural code} on $N$ neurons is a function $c:[N]\to\{0,1\}$ assigning
each neuron a binary state ($1$ = firing, $0$ = silent), i.e.\ an element of the
Boolean cube $\{0,1\}^N$. We write $\PN=\{0,1\}^N$ for the set of all patterns.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Silent pattern]
The \emph{silent} pattern $\zero\in\PN$ is the constant function
$\zero(i)=0$ for all $i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Weight]
The \emph{weight} of a pattern $c$, written $\wt(c)$, is the number of firing
neurons, $\wt(c)=\bigl|\{i\in[N]: c(i)=1\}\bigr|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hamming distance]
For patterns $x,y\in\PN$, the \emph{Hamming distance} $\dH(x,y)$ is the number of
neurons on which they disagree, $\dH(x,y)=\bigl|\{i\in[N]: x(i)\ne y(i)\}\bigr|$.
\end{definition}

The Hamming distance is a metric on $\PN$: nonnegative, symmetric, zero exactly
when $x=y$, and satisfying the triangle inequality
$\dH(x,z)\le \dH(x,y)+\dH(y,z)$. It equals the number of single-neuron flips
needed to turn $x$ into $y$, which is why it models noise: a pattern corrupted by
$m$ misfires lies at distance exactly $m$ from the original.

\section{Model: noise-tolerant codebooks}

\begin{definition}[Codebook]
A \emph{codebook} is a finite set $C\subseteq\PN$ of patterns; its elements are
\emph{codewords}, the patterns to which the population assigns meaning.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$t$-error-correcting codebook]
A codebook $C$ \emph{corrects $t$ errors} if any two distinct codewords are at
Hamming distance at least $2t+1$:
\[
  \forall\, x,y\in C,\ x\ne y \implies \dH(x,y)\ge 2t+1.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Decodability]
If $C$ corrects $t$ errors and $x\in C$ is transmitted and corrupted by at most
$t$ neuron flips, producing $z$ with $\dH(x,z)\le t$, then $x$ is the unique
codeword nearest to $z$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Let $y\in C$, $y\ne x$. By the triangle inequality and minimum distance,
$2t+1\le \dH(x,y)\le \dH(x,z)+\dH(z,y)\le t+\dH(z,y)$, so
$\dH(z,y)\ge t+1>t\ge \dH(x,z)$. Thus $x$ is strictly closer to $z$ than any
other codeword.
\end{proof}

\section{Raw capacity and the weight partition}

\begin{theorem}[Raw capacity]\label{thm:raw}
The number of neural codes on $N$ neurons is $|\PN|=2^N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each of the $N$ neurons is assigned one of two states independently, giving
$2^N$ patterns.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Distance from silence equals weight]\label{lem:wt}
For every pattern $c$, $\dH(\zero,c)=\wt(c)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The silent pattern disagrees with $c$ exactly on the neurons where $c(i)=1$, of
which there are $\wt(c)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sparse count]\label{thm:sparse}
For $0\le k\le N$, the number of patterns of weight exactly $k$ is $\binom{N}{k}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A weight-$k$ pattern is determined by which $k$ of the $N$ neurons fire; this is
a bijection with the $k$-element subsets of $[N]$, of which there are
$\binom{N}{k}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Weight partition]\label{cor:partition}
$\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{N}\binom{N}{k}=2^N.$
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Every pattern has a unique weight in $\{0,\dots,N\}$; summing the sparse counts
of Theorem~\ref{thm:sparse} counts every pattern once, giving $|\PN|=2^N$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:raw}.
\end{proof}

\section{Hamming balls and their volume}

\begin{definition}[Hamming ball]
For a pattern $c$ and radius $r\ge0$, the \emph{Hamming ball} is
$B(c,r)=\{x\in\PN:\dH(c,x)\le r\}$: the patterns obtainable from $c$ by flipping
at most $r$ neurons.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Translation invariance]\label{lem:trans}
For any $c,x$, $\dH(c,x)=\dH(\zero,\,c\oplus x)$, where $c\oplus x$ is the
neuron-wise exclusive-or.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Both sides count the neurons on which $c$ and $x$ differ: $c\oplus x$ fires
exactly there, and its distance from silence equals its weight, the same count.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Center independence]\label{thm:center}
For any center $c$ and radius $r$, $|B(c,r)|=|B(\zero,r)|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The map $x\mapsto c\oplus x$ is an involution of $\PN$ and, by
Lemma~\ref{lem:trans}, carries $B(c,r)$ bijectively onto $B(\zero,r)$, since
$\dH(c,x)\le r\iff \dH(\zero,c\oplus x)\le r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Ball volume]\label{thm:volume}
For any center $c$ and radius $r$,
$|B(c,r)|=\sum_{k=0}^{r}\binom{N}{k}=:V(N,r)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:center} count $B(\zero,r)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:wt},
$x\in B(\zero,r)\iff \wt(x)\le r$, so $B(\zero,r)$ is the disjoint union of the
weight-$k$ patterns for $k=0,\dots,r$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:sparse} these number
$\binom{N}{k}$, and summing gives $\sum_{k=0}^{r}\binom{N}{k}$.
\end{proof}

Note $V(N,0)=1$ and $V(N,N)=2^N$, consistent with
Corollary~\ref{cor:partition}.

\section{The sphere-packing bound}

\begin{lemma}[Disjoint balls]\label{lem:disjoint}
If $C$ corrects $t$ errors, the balls $\{B(c,t):c\in C\}$ are pairwise disjoint.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $z\in B(x,t)\cap B(y,t)$ with $x\ne y$ in $C$, then
$\dH(x,y)\le \dH(x,z)+\dH(z,y)\le 2t$, contradicting $\dH(x,y)\ge 2t+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sphere-packing / Hamming bound]\label{thm:hamming}
Let $C$ be a codebook on $N$ neurons that corrects $t$ errors. Then
\[
  |C|\cdot\sum_{k=0}^{t}\binom{N}{k}\;\le\;2^N.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:disjoint} the balls $B(c,t)$, $c\in C$, are pairwise disjoint,
so
\[
  \Bigl|\bigcup_{c\in C}B(c,t)\Bigr|
  =\sum_{c\in C}|B(c,t)|
  =|C|\cdot V(N,t),
\]
using Theorem~\ref{thm:volume}. The union lies in $\PN$, which has $2^N$ elements
(Theorem~\ref{thm:raw}), so $|C|\cdot\sum_{k=0}^{t}\binom{N}{k}\le 2^N$.
\end{proof}

\section{Consequences}

\begin{corollary}[Recovery of raw capacity]
For $t=0$, Theorem~\ref{thm:hamming} reads $|C|\le 2^N$, recovering
Theorem~\ref{thm:raw} as the zero-noise special case.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Price of single-error correction]
If $C$ corrects a single error (distinct codewords differ in at least $3$
neurons), then $V(N,1)=N+1$ and
\[
  |C|\cdot(N+1)\le 2^N,\qquad |C|\le\frac{2^N}{N+1}.
\]
\end{corollary}

More generally, the rate $R=\tfrac1N\log_2|C|$ of a $t$-error code satisfies
$R\le 1-\tfrac1N\log_2\sum_{k=0}^{t}\binom{N}{k}$, a quantitative
capacity--robustness trade-off.

\section{Numerical illustration}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}
\toprule
$N$ & $2^N$ & $V(N,1)$ & $2^N/(N{+}1)$ & $V(N,2)$ & $2^N/V(N,2)$\\
\midrule
3  & 8        & 4  & 2.00      & 7   & 1.14\\
7  & 128      & 8  & 16.00     & 29  & 4.41\\
15 & 32768    & 16 & 2048.00   & 121 & 270.8\\
31 & $2^{31}$ & 32 & 67108864  & 497 & $4.32\times10^{6}$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The row $N=7$ is special: $2^7/8=16$ is an integer, attained by the
Hamming$(7,4)$ code, a single-error-correcting codebook of exactly $16$
codewords. Codes meeting the bound with equality are \emph{perfect}: their
radius-$t$ balls tile the entire cube. Perfect codes are rare --- the Hamming
codes and the binary Golay code are the principal examples.

\section{Interpretation for population coding}

The bound recasts a biological intuition as a theorem. The brain favors
distributed population codes over fragile single-neuron representations. Here a
distributed code is one with large minimum Hamming distance --- exactly the
error-correction condition. The bound makes the trade explicit: tolerating $t$
faults forces each concept to claim a private ball of volume $V(N,t)$, and only
$2^N/V(N,t)$ such balls fit. A population cannot be simultaneously maximally
expressive and maximally robust; the exchange rate is a sum of binomial
coefficients --- the same geometry that governs engineered channels.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The bound is an upper bound on codebook size. Complementary directions:
\emph{Gilbert--Varshamov} lower bounds ($|C|\ge 2^N/V(N,2t)$ by a greedy
argument); the \emph{Singleton} bound ($|C|\le 2^{N-d+1}$) and MDS neural codes;
\emph{perfect codes} and the Hamming$(7,4)$ equality case; \emph{Plotkin} and
linear-programming bounds for the high-noise regime; metric-space packaging of
the pattern space; and energy-optimal robust codes combining distance with
sparse-coding costs.

\section{Conclusion}

The raw representational capacity of $N$ binary neurons is $2^N$; demanding
robustness to $t$ misfires compresses the usable capacity to at most
$2^N/\sum_{k=0}^{t}\binom{N}{k}$ via the sphere-packing bound. The argument is
elementary --- a weight partition, a translation symmetry of the Boolean cube,
and the triangle inequality --- yet it captures a fundamental limit: in any noisy
binary substrate, expressiveness and reliability trade off at a rate set by the
volume of a Hamming ball, and the raw capacity $2^N$ is the $t=0$ shadow of this
deeper law.

